ceos and founder ceos are by far the  richest people in the world while there  are some oddballs on the billionaires  list who are directors investors and  even athletes the vast majority are  company leaders  unlike most jobs though company leaders  are rarely paid in cash many of the top  ceos take a cash salary of just one  dollar per year and even for the ceos  that take a moderate cash salary the  cash portion is an extremely small  portion of their total compensation for  example out of their pitch's 281 million  salary in 2019 only 2 million was paid  in cash considering this how do  billionaires even access the  extraordinary wealth they have without  crashing their company's stock and what  about founder ceos that own 40 or 50 of  a massive conglomerate for example  bernard arnold owns 47 of lvmh which  gives him a mind-boggling net worth of  123 billion dollars but is it even  possible for him to cash out well at his  age the answer is probably not but  here's how billionaires access their  paper fortunes and how they live a rich  lifestyle without cash  the first thing to note about most of  these billionaires compensation  structure is that they can't cash out  their salary even if they wanted to this  is especially applicable to non-founder  ceos who don't already have a lot of  equity you see the last thing board  members want is for a ceo who either  quits or is fired under negative  circumstances to dump all of their  shares and crash the stock on their way  out so a restriction they implement to  protect against this is stock vesting  the first type of stock vesting is  time-based stock vesting time-based  stock vesting is usually most common  amongst non-founder ceos as the name  suggests these ceos are restricted from  selling any of their stock compensation  for a set period of time this rule  doesn't just apply to executives either  employees at these companies are  generally subject to similar  restrictions both google and apple for  instance follow a four-year vesting  schedule with a one-year investing clip  for employees the vesting cliff simply  means that you can't sell any of your  stock within the first year and after  that you'll get access to your stock on  a linear basis over the next four years  so really you won't get access to all of  your stock until five years after you're  given that compensation and if you leave  during this time period you of course  lose access to your remaining stock ceos  follow a similar structure but their  investing periods are way larger given  that their compensation is also way  larger for example when tim cook was  hired as ceo in 2011 they gave them 1  million shares of apple but the stock  vesting period was 10 years the first  half million shares would unlock in  august of 2016 and the other half  million would unlock in august of 2021.  this is why we saw insane headlines  about tim cook receiving 750 million  worth of stock last year while this is  technically true it's also kind of  misleading when tim was awarded these  shares in 2011 they were worth 384  million dollars and given a vesting  period of 10 years this basically  translates to 38.4 million dollars per  year which is still an insane amount  but it's nowhere close to 750 million  dollars in a single year  tim actually went ahead and sold this  entire compensation right after he got  it but thanks to the vesting period  apple stock had grown to a point at  which it could easily sustain the  selling pressure and protect shareholder  value  the other most common type of stock  vesting is milestone-based vesting  instead of tying up stock access to a  time period this option ties up stock  access to various milestones this type  of vesting is most common amongst  founders ceos who already have loads of  equity given that they already have  plenty of shares it's not the board  member's top concern to get these ceos  even more invested in the stock for  example bernard arnold already owns 47  percent of lvmh so pumping up his stake  to 55 or 60 probably wouldn't motivate  him that much more  as a result board members are able to be  much more strict with their compensation  plans requiring these ceos to not only  put up time but put up results a great  example of milestone-based investing is  elon musk and tesla  elon musk receives no salary no bonus  and no time-based equity the only way  for him to earn more stock is to reach  the milestones outlined in this  compensation plan  as you can see these plans consist of  both market cap milestones and  operational milestones and he must hit  both of these numbers to be compensated  when these milestones were established  in 2018 tesla's market cap was just 59  billion dollars so reaching a 650  billion market cap was quite an  ambitious goal but elon has since  crossed these numbers which has given  him paydays worth tens of billions it  should also be noted though that if he  didn't reach his ambitious milestones  within 10 years he would have gotten no  compensation for 10 years of work this  is obviously rather harsh so many  companies tend to go for a more softened  approach they'll often combine  time-based vesting with milestone-based  vesting to create a hybrid vesting model  this way ceos don't have to achieve  insane growth numbers to be paid but at  the same time they can't just stand back  their time at the top either for example  this was one of the caveats that apple  introduced to tim's compensation plan in  2013. they clarified that apple must  also beat the s p 500 for tim to receive  his full compensation for a company like  apple beating the s p 500 throughout the  2010s wasn't that challenging of a goal  but it did introduce some level of  accountability  alright so companies use time and a  milestone based vesting to keep  executives loyal to the company over the  long term but what about during the  meantime for example from tim cook's  perspective it's great that he could  receive up to 1 million apple shares in  10 years but how can you enjoy the  benefits of that right now  this is obviously a rich person problem  but the solution is rather interesting  to discuss  the answer to this problem is usually  debt while tim couldn't sell most of the  stock he received in 2011 he could use  it as collateral  now trying to collateralize rsu income  or restricted stock income is not the  easiest of tasks for us normal people  but for company leaders this is rather  easy and it's not just because they're  in a powerful position the main reason  is that the items that they're trying to  buy are usually a small fraction of  their income for example tim recently  bought a 9.1 million house i have a  feeling that he just paid cash for this  property given that he bought this house  right after his stock compensation  unlocked but it wouldn't have been that  hard for him to take out a mortgage  either given that the house cost less  than 1 30 of his compensation plan  similarly elon took out 61 million worth  of mortgages in 2019 which was likely a  large amount compared to his cash  reserves but a miniscule portion  compared to its net worth this reasoning  obviously does not apply to the average  person who is buying a house that's  worth two to five times their gross  income for billionaires though this  strategy doesn't just apply to housing  it applies to basically every facet of  their life whether they're trying to buy  a private jet or twitter  also this strategy isn't just a  workaround stock vesting periods either  there's loads of benefits to leveraging  debt from maximizing returns and  minimizing taxes to benefiting from  inflation so these stock vesting periods  are just even more reason to take out  debt for these billionaires  everything we've covered so far accounts  for the vast majority of ceos it's  usually just a case of borrowing against  your rsus to fund your lifestyle until  you unlock your compensation packages  there are a few dozen people in the  world however who have significantly  more power for example no one can  prevent jeff bezos bill gates or elon  musk from selling their initial stakes  in the company so technically these guys  could try to sell all of their stock in  one goal and crash the market but given  that these guys are founders this is  extremely rare but it does happen from  time to time  and i don't think you'd be surprised to  hear that one of the ceos that was bold  enough to do this was steve jobs in 1985  steve jobs got entangled in months of  vicious battles with their ceo at the  time john scully and as you would guess  this eventually resulted in him being  ousted in september of 1985. out of rage  steve would go ahead and sell his entire  11 steak in apple except for one share  looking back at apple stock during the  time it doesn't actually look like apple  fell that much when steve actually sold  rather most of the sell-off took place  leading up to steve being fired by the  time steve departed the company the  stock was down a painful 77 from their  all-time high so while steve selling his  entire stake was a monumental event his  sale itself didn't impact the stock too  much given that most of the fear was  already priced in another example of a  foundry selling out is travis kalanick  and uber like steve jobs travis was  ousted from uber after months of  boardroom battles in december of 2019  and he of course sold all of his talk  right after he was ousted what's  interesting though is that he also sold  near the bottom just like steve jobs now  uber is at even lower levels today but  that's besides the point  the point is that by the time a founder  sells all of their shares in a rage fit  the stock is already in the toilet so  their selling itself doesn't hurt it all  that much if they were to just randomly  sell out of the blue though the stock  would no doubt get obliterated  aside from loose cannon examples most  founder ceos cash out extremely  strategically for example jeff bezos  sells a few billion dollars worth of  stock every year but he doesn't do so  without months of warning and he sells  in small digestible batches so the stock  price is really affected by his sales  likely the closest example to randomly  selling out of the blue is elon's recent  sale for twitter  in late april elon sold 8.5 billion  worth of tesla stock for about one  percent of the company this led to tesla  stocks selling off about 10 by the time  he finished selling this means that  selling one percent of the company  correlated to about a 10 sell-off so if  a founder was to sell 10 out of the blue  that would likely cause the stock to  crash 70 or 80 percent and that's not  even taking into account that the  selloff will accelerate as the foundry  continues to sell the only way for a  founder to sell out of such a position  is to spend decades slowly selling their  stock just like bill gates did when  microsoft went public in 1986 bill gates  owned 45 percent of microsoft today he  owns just 1.34 of the company now this  1.34 stake is still worth 25 billion  dollars but given that bill gates has  over 100 billion dollars the vast  majority of his wealth is no longer in  microsoft this makes him one of the most  liquid billionaires in the world but to  get to this position it took him at 36  years and if any other mega cap founder  wants to cash out without destroying  their stock this is really the only  option they have as well  and that's how ceos cash out of their  massive positions if you were the  founder of a mega cap company would you  holler your stick to your grave or would  you slowly sell off like bill comment  that down below also drop a like if you  wish you had rich people problems  instead and of course consider checking  out our international channels to watch  our videos in other languages and  consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm hari and i'll see you guys on  the next one
 

